         <p>What topics get you excited? What do you spend your free time doing? What subjects in your courses did you genuinely enjoy learning about? A career that aligns with your passions won't feel like work; it will feel like a mission. Make a list of your top five passions, no matter how unrelated they seem to your degree. For example, a student studying <a data-preview="" href="https://www.google.com/search?ved=1t:260882&amp;q=what+is+computer+science+degree&amp;bbid=8491425780932094856&amp;bpid=9106828125350114165" target="_blank">computer science</a> might have a passion for <a data-preview="" href="https://www.google.com/search?ved=1t:260882&amp;q=careers+combining+photography+and+computer+science&amp;bbid=8491425780932094856&amp;bpid=9106828125350114165" target="_blank">photography</a>. This could lead to a career in <a data-preview="" href="https://www.google.com/search?ved=1t:260882&amp;q=define+UI/UX+design&amp;bbid=8491425780932094856&amp;bpid=9106828125350114165" target="_blank">UI/UX design</a> or creating software for photo editing, blending a passion with a profession.</p>
 
         <h3>Step 2: Understand Your Core Strengths and Skills</h3>
-        <p>What are you good at? What skills have you developed, both inside and outside the classroom? This goes beyond academic knowledge. Think about your <a data-preview="" href="https://www.google.com/search?ved=1t:260882&amp;q=define+soft+skills&amp;bbid=8491425780932094856&amp;bpid=9106828125350114165" target="_blank">soft skills</a>, which are crucial for success in any career. Are you a great communicator? A natural leader? A creative problem-solver? You can also refer to the skills you’ve been building over your student life, such as the time management and productivity techniques from our previous post on <a href="https://studenttimepk.blogspot.com/2025/08/the-ultimate-time-management.html">time management hacks</a>.</p>
+        <p>What are you good at? What skills have you developed, both inside and outside the classroom? This goes beyond academic knowledge. Think about your <a data-preview="" href="https://www.google.com/search?ved=1t:260882&amp;q=define+soft+skills&amp;bbid=8491425780932094856&amp;bpid=9106828125350114165" target="_blank">soft skills</a>, which are crucial for success in any career. Are you a great communicator? A natural leader? A creative problem-solver? You can also refer to the skills you’ve been building over your student life, such as the time management and productivity techniques from our previous post on <a href="https://studenttimepk.blogspot.com/2025/08/the-ultimate-time-management-blueprint.html">time management hacks</a>.</p>
         
         <h3>Step 3: Define Your Values and Vision for Life</h3>
         <p>What is most important to you? Is it making a lot of money? Having a good work-life balance? Making a positive social impact? Having autonomy and flexibility? Your values should be the guiding principles for your career choices. A high-paying job that goes against your core values will never make you truly happy. Visualize your ideal life in 5, 10, and 20 years. What do you see? This vision is your north star.</p>
