         <p>Don't just look for job titles; research entire industries. What are the major players in your field? What are the key trends and future projections? Use platforms like <a data-preview="" href="https://www.google.com/search?ved=1t:260882&amp;q=LinkedIn&amp;bbid=8491425780932094856&amp;bpid=9106828125350114165" target="_blank">LinkedIn</a>, industry-specific forums, and job boards to see what's out there. Pay attention to the skills and qualifications required for the roles that interest you. This research phase is similar to the active learning we talked about in our guide to <a href="https://studenttimepk.blogspot.com/2025/08/beyond-highlighting-ultimate-guide-to.html">note-taking and study strategies</a>—it's about digging deep and understanding the landscape.</p>
 
         <h3>Step 2: Informational Interviews – The Secret to Insider Knowledge</h3>
-        <p>An <a data-preview="" href="https://www.google.com/search?ved=1t:260882&amp;q=how+to+conduct+informational+interview&amp;bbid=8491425780932094856&amp;bpid=9106828125350114165" target="_blank">informational interview</a> is a powerful but underutilized tool. This is a short, informal conversation with a professional working in a field that interests you. The goal is not to ask for a job, but to learn from their experience. Ask them about their daily tasks, what they enjoy most about their work, what challenges they face, and what advice they have for a student like you. These conversations provide invaluable insights that you won't find on a company website. Use your communication skills from our post on <a href="https://studenttimepk.blogspot.com/2025/08/your-guide-to-a-calm-mind-navigating.html">communication and collaboration</a> to make a great first impression and build a genuine connection.</p>
+        <p>An <a data-preview="" href="https://www.google.com/search?ved=1t:260882&amp;q=how+to+conduct+informational+interview&amp;bbid=8491425780932094856&amp;bpid=9106828125350114165" target="_blank">informational interview</a> is a powerful but underutilized tool. This is a short, informal conversation with a professional working in a field that interests you. The goal is not to ask for a job, but to learn from their experience. Ask them about their daily tasks, what they enjoy most about their work, what challenges they face, and what advice they have for a student like you. These conversations provide invaluable insights that you won't find on a company website. Use your communication skills from our post on communication and collaboration to make a great first impression and build a genuine connection.</p>
         
         <h3>Step 3: Hands-On Experience: Internships and Volunteer Work</h3>
         <p>Nothing teaches you more about a career than actually doing it. Internships, part-time jobs, and volunteer work are essential for gaining practical experience and testing a career path. They allow you to apply the knowledge you’ve gained in your studies and understand the day-to-day realities of a profession. Internships also give you a chance to build your professional network and secure a job offer after graduation. This practical experience is the best way to prove you can do the job and to build confidence in your skills. A summer internship or volunteer role is a great way to put your learning into practice.</p>
